# ISSUE-33 revision test plan for PR #48

The delta covers the proof assertion and production bundle failures from check 138862675581. Acceptance indices follow `WORKUNIT.md:26`–`:32`. `ORIGINAL_TEST_PLAN.md` supplies the approved coverage outside these changes. The revision adds evidence for the integrated build and both browser journeys. [Browser tests run 45512255292](https://github.com/supernovae/signal-lost/actions/runs/45512255292)

## Acceptance mapping

| Criterion | Revision verification and original coverage |
| --- | --- |
| A1, solver, evidence and exits | Reuse original A1 and D1 for the solver on the same engine and story inputs. RD1 compares the adventure delivered in the built content with the authored, validated adventure. |
| A2, reference and reachability diagnostics | Preserve original A2 and D1/D2 for unknown and unreachable references. RD1 runs the existing invalid-build diagnostic case and compares valid output to the shared loader. |
| A3, fixture-only second adventure | Reuse original A3 and D1 for the fixture through the registry. RD1 compares every shipped content collection and the manifest to the shared loader run over `content/`. The tiny test fixture must be absent from the built doors. |
| A4, distinct persisted rewards | Reuse original A4 and D1 for the persisted reward facts. RC1 exercises both ending texts and their restoration in the keyboard and pointer paths, which the previous custody timeout prevented. |
| A5, mid-adventure reload and keyboard/pointer controls | Retain original A5 Node coverage. RC1 drives both fixed browser journeys through the preserved partial shelf reload and re-entry, action controls and ending restore on the production build. |
| A6, keyboard ending and BBS return | RC1 reruns the keyboard path past the new proof assertion to the indexed-copy ending, EXIT, focus and working signed-in BBS navigation. Reuse the remainder of original A6. |
| A7, either verified route and learned procedure | RD1 reruns the existing #35 evidence, independent flags, and shelf handoff cases. RC1 asserts fresh route-specific output and persisted shared and route-specific proof immediately after submission, then exercises the early and wrong-order responses, final inspection and correct order, and notebook after exit and reload on both routes. Reuse original A7 checks for all incomplete subsets, wrong permutations and persisted door reward details. |

## Developer-owned focused checks

Use the selected isolated native Codex backend on Linux and Node 24. Run local checks with the prepared dependencies and no browser. Record exact commands, result, elapsed time, and the candidate SHA or source fingerprint in `IMPLEMENTATION.md`. Store detailed logs in scratch when needed. Fingerprint the checked source, tests, fixtures, content and lockfile/config inputs where Git metadata is unavailable.

| ID | Check | Expected duration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| RD1 | Focused Q17 and content entry-point suites below. The production Vite test compares all normalized content and the emitted manifest, with the existing invalid-input diagnostic test. | Under 2 min total |
| RD2 | Changed-file ESLint and Prettier plus Playwright collection with `--list`. Collection exercises spec loading without starting Chromium. The production build supplies TypeScript diagnostics. | Under 2 min total |
| RD3 | `npm run build` and the Node asset check below, measured on the real production entry. Record before and after file names, gzip bytes and KiB, environment size, and manifest version and entry count. | Under 2 min |

```sh
npm test -- tests/unit/bbs.test.ts -t 'the Q17 investigation'
npm test -- tests/unit/content.test.ts -t 'entry points'
npm exec -- eslint scripts/content-plugin.ts src/content/browser.ts src/main.ts tests/unit/content.test.ts tests/e2e/bbs.spec.ts
npm exec -- prettier --check scripts/content-plugin.ts src/content/browser.ts src/main.ts tests/unit/content.test.ts tests/e2e/bbs.spec.ts docs/architecture.md
npm exec -- playwright test --list --project=chromium tests/e2e/bbs.spec.ts tests/e2e/budget.spec.ts tests/e2e/smoke.spec.ts tests/e2e/startup.spec.ts
npm run build
```

For RD1, make the Vite test run `contentPlugin()` with `configFile: false`, `write: false` and the browser content module as an ES library entry. Inspect the emitted entry and asset. Require zero entry imports, import the actual emitted code using a base64 JavaScript data URL, and compare `loadBundledContent()` and the parsed manifest to `loadContent(readContentDir('content'))`. This catches missing collections, defaults and community expansion that a browser journey would only partly exercise. Confirm the shipped doors and absence of the tiny fixture through this parity check and the original A3 assertions.

For RD3, take the before measurement from a clean production build of the revision input, or cite the matching published-head CI and planner baseline (64,171 gzip bytes). Compare the candidate with the same calculation and default Pages base. Check that the budgeted file is the HTML module entry, that the stylesheet exists, and that the lazy BBS connection and environment chunks are emitted. Run this in the build root after a successful build.

```sh
node --input-type=module <<'NODE' || exit 1
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';

const html = readFileSync('dist/index.html', 'utf8');
const assets = readdirSync('dist/assets');
const entry = html.match(/<script\b[^>]*\btype="module"[^>]*\bsrc="([^"]+)"/)?.[1];
const style = html.match(/<link\b[^>]*\brel="stylesheet"[^>]*\bhref="([^"]+)"/)?.[1];
assert.match(entry ?? '', /^\/signal-lost\/assets\/index-[^/]+\.js$/);
assert.match(style ?? '', /^\/signal-lost\/assets\/index-[^/]+\.css$/);
assert(assets.includes(style.split('/').at(-1)));

for (const [prefix, limit] of [['index-', 60], ['environment-', 170]]) {
  const matches = assets.filter((name) => name.startsWith(prefix) && name.endsWith('.js'));
  assert.equal(matches.length, 1, prefix);
  const [name] = matches;
  if (prefix === 'index-') assert.equal(entry, '/signal-lost/assets/' + name);
  const bytes = gzipSync(readFileSync('dist/assets/' + name)).length;
  const kib = bytes / 1024;
  console.log(name, bytes + ' bytes gzip', kib + ' KiB');
  assert(kib <= limit, name + ' exceeds ' + limit + ' KiB');
}
assert(assets.some((name) => /^connection-.+\.js$/.test(name)));
const manifest = JSON.parse(readFileSync('dist/content-manifest.json', 'utf8'));
console.log('Manifest', manifest.contentVersion, manifest.entries.length + ' entries');
NODE
for asset in dist/assets/*.js; do node --check "$asset" || exit 1; done
```

The published-head startup and keyboard passes provide context. Use the revised candidate's CI run for acceptance.

## Tester-owned independent review

- Compare each browser proof with the authored presentation, sender, title, exact verified line and associated event. Confirm the assertions address the newly appended grouped output, that the saved proof belongs to that route, and that both journeys continue straight into the directory and the existing shelf/ending/reload checks. Check the standalone #35 journey and gameplay source against the revision input.
- Trace the build-only module through the configured production plugin and the development fallback. Verify single-build validation and manifest emission, full normalized payload, which module the real HTML loads, and continued availability of the lazy BBS and environment assets. Check the candidate's gzip calculation and threshold file and verify the shipped content and test fixture boundary.
- Reuse passing RD1–RD3 from the matching candidate or source fingerprint and applicable original coverage. Repeat only the smallest affected check for a relevant source change, observed failure, or unresolved coverage. Record the reason and evidence in `VALIDATION.md`. Mark revised-browser acceptance pending external evidence until the matching CI run is available.

## Coordinator policy and browser CI

The coordinator owns the complete trusted-base `.swarm/validation/required-checks.yaml` policy once after role work on the final candidate and before publication. Agent evidence is advisory. Chromium execution belongs to Browser tests CI on Ubuntu using the existing Playwright setup and intercepted `/signal-lost/` production build.

| ID | Command or check | Sole owner | Expected duration | Evidence and later reuse |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| RV1 | Execute the complete trusted-base format, lint, build and test policy, including `npm ci --prefer-offline --no-audit --no-fund && npm test`. | coordinator | 5–15 min with prepared dependencies, 15 min limit for the unit command. | Coordinator record and authoritative validation report for the final candidate and policy identity. Maintainer and publication reuse it. |
| RC1 | Existing Browser tests workflow with `npm ci`, Chromium setup and `npm run test:e2e`. Confirm the standalone #35 header test, both #33 route-to-ending journeys, the budget and software WebGL checks, Pages smoke and startup no-error/no-off-site checks. | CI | 5–15 min including setup and build, 30 min workflow limit. | Actions run and job URL, revision SHA or merge SHA containing it, and named Playwright results. Tester, coordinator and maintainer reuse a matching passing run for browser acceptance. |

On the revision input, the planner ran the Q17 Node suite and collected the 15 Playwright cases in the focused files with `--list`. The RD3 asset command rejected the initial entry at 62.6669921875 KiB and accepted the separate scratch probe at 58.150390625 KiB. RD1–RD3 and RC1 supply the results for the integrated revision.

